  "ahmedabad": [
    "Ahmedabad sees weddings across a mix of settings — banquet halls, hotel lawns, rooftop venues and home courtyards — and each one changes how the tying schedule is planned. A hotel booking usually means a fixed preparation room and predictable access, while a home or courtyard function often needs the team to adapt around family rituals happening in parallel. We ask about the venue type early so the right number of artists and the right pace are set from the start, rather than guessing on the day. Peak wedding months in and around Ahmedabad bring tighter artist availability, so dates during the busiest weeks benefit from earlier confirmation. Off-season and weekday bookings tend to have more flexibility on timing and can sometimes accommodate later confirmations. Either way, the core planning stays the same: confirm the property, the room being used for styling, and roughly how many people need to be ready before the first ritual or the baraat departure. Weather is a smaller factor for most Ahmedabad bookings since safa tying happens indoors or under cover, but for any outdoor portrait session immediately after styling, we factor in extra time so fabric and finishing hold up under natural light.",
    "Picking the right package for a Ahmedabad wedding usually comes down to three numbers: how many people need a safa, how much time is available before the ceremony, and how detailed the groom's own styling needs to be. A groom-only booking is the simplest option and works well for smaller functions or when family members already have their own arrangements. A groom-plus-family package adds a coordinated look for parents and siblings without the scale of a full baraati booking. The complete package — groom, family and baraat — is the most common choice for larger Ahmedabad weddings, since it keeps the whole wedding party visually consistent in photographs and keeps the tying schedule managed by one team rather than several. Fabric quality is a separate decision from group size: some families choose a premium fabric for the groom and a simpler, more economical option for the wider baraat, which keeps costs sensible without compromising the groom's look. Whichever package you're considering, sharing your rough budget alongside the guest count helps us recommend something realistic rather than the largest available option by default. We're happy to talk through two or three package options before you decide.",
  ],
